2025-09-12 13:12:14 - src.vue_api_endpoints - INFO - [Request 0866ca46-f37f-4918-8dd8-d1f28bc6c0c2] JSON parsing successful: {'type': 'text', 'text': '# MEMORANDUM: Whether Florida requires two witnesses to sign a lease\n\n## Question Presented\n\nIs there a Florida requirement that two witnesses sign a lease, and how does Florida Second District Court of Appeal (2d DCA) case law bear on the issue?\n\n## Short Answer\n\nNo—since **July 1, 2020**, Florida law no longer requires any subscribing witnesses for a lease (residential or commercial) or any instrument “pertaining to a lease.” That change came via an amendment to **Fla. Stat. § 689.01(1)** (HB 469). Before July 1, 2020, the statute did require two subscribing witnesses (as to the lessor’s execution), and older cases wrestled with whether an unwitnessed long-term lease was void or enforceable in equity. Although the 2d DCA did not squarely decide a *lease* witness case, its decisions on related § 689.01 witness issues are frequently cited and show a consistent willingness to enforce instruments despite technical witness defects where equity favors enforcement. In particular:\n\n* **Medina v. Orange County** (2d DCA 1962) upheld a deed where one witness subscribed post-delivery because two people *actually witnessed* execution; it relied on **Gill v. Livingston** (Fla. 1947), which estopped owners from voiding a lease executed with only one witness after accepting its benefits. ([CaseMine][1])\n* **Carroll v. Dougherty** (2d DCA 1978) held § 689.01’s witness rule governs *conveyances*, not *contracts* to convey; after the 1968 Constitution removed “duly executed,” two witnesses were no longer required on contracts to sell homestead property. ([CaseMine][2])\n* **Wickes Corp. v. Moxley** (2d DCA 1977), affirmed by the Florida Supreme Court (1978), reached the same conclusion for homestead mortgages (no two-witness requirement post-1968 Constitution). ([Leagle][3])\n\n## Background & Statutory Framework\n\n1. **Current text.** Section 689.01(1) still requires two witnesses for many real-property conveyances, **but expressly provides**: “no subscribing witnesses shall be required for a lease of real property or any such instrument pertaining to a lease of real property.” (Effective **July 1, 2020**.) ([Online Sunshine][4])\n\n   * The 2020 Senate Judiciary staff analysis confirms the change eliminates the two-witness requirement for leases and notes that historically only the **lessor’s** signature had to be witnessed. ([The Florida Senate][5])\n\n2. **Electronic witnessing (other instruments).** Separate 2019 and 2020 amendments allow witnessing by audio-video technology for instruments that still require witnesses. ([Online Sunshine][4])\n\n3. **Recording considerations.** Recording statutes (e.g., **§ 695.26**) impose formatting and name/address requirements if an instrument *has* witnesses; leases may be recorded without witnesses after 2020, but if witnesses appear on a recordable instrument, their printed names/addresses are required. ([Online Sunshine][6])\n\n## Analysis\n\n### A. Leases executed **on or after July 1, 2020**\n\nThere is **no** two-witness requirement—period. The Online Sunshine version of § 689.01(1) contains the controlling proviso, and multiple contemporaneous advisories from Florida firms summarize the change the same way. ([Online Sunshine][4])\n\n### B. Pre-July 1, 2020 leases (legacy contracts)\n\nBefore the amendment, § 689.01 required two subscribing witnesses for leases longer than one year (as to the **lessor’s** signature). Courts split on the consequences of noncompliance: some decisions (not from the 2d DCA) treated unwitnessed leases as void, while others enforced them via equitable doctrines (estoppel/part performance) when the parties acted under the lease.\n\n* **Florida Supreme Court—Gill v. Livingston (1947):** Lessors who placed the lessee in possession and accepted benefits were **estopped** from voiding a lease executed with only one subscribing witness. Gill is repeatedly cited for this equitable limit on using the witness defect as a sword. ([CourtListener][7])\n\n* **2d DCA—Medina v. Orange County (1962):** Although a deed case, Medina’s treatment of § 689.01 is instructive. The court held the statute does **not** require witnesses to sign in the grantor’s presence or before delivery; a witness who actually observed execution may subscribe later. Medina rests on Gill and equity and is commonly used to resist hyper-technical witness attacks. ([CaseMine][1])\n\n* **2d DCA—Carroll v. Dougherty (1978):** Clarifies § 689.01 applies to **instruments of conveyance**, not to **contracts** to convey (after 1968’s removal of “duly executed”). This narrowing is frequently cited to prevent importing the two-witness formality into agreements that are not themselves conveyances. While not a lease case, it shows the 2d DCA’s reluctance to expand § 689.01 beyond its text. ([CaseMine][2])\n\n* **2d DCA—Wickes Corp. v. Moxley (1977), aff’d, 356 So. 2d 785 (Fla. 1978):** Same textual approach for homestead mortgages—no two-witness requirement under the post-1968 Constitution. Again, the 2d DCA favored the statute’s text and rejected extra-textual witness formalities. ([Leagle][3])\n\n* **Legislative codification in 2020:** The Judiciary staff analysis both recounts this background and cites **Gill** and **Taylor v. Rosman** (3d DCA) as examples of equitable estoppel stopping parties from voiding unwitnessed leases after they acted under them (e.g., possession/rent payments). The amendment then removed the witness requirement for **any** lease going forward. ([The Florida Senate][5])\n\n### C. Practical effects in the **Second DCA**\n\n* **Today:** No witnesses are required for leases; use ordinary execution formalities (and notarization if recording is desired) and comply with **§ 695.26** formatting if recording. ([Online Sunshine][4])\n* **Old leases:** If a pre-July 2020 lease in a 2d DCA venue lacked two witnesses, you have strong equitable arguments under **Medina**/**Gill** to avoid forfeiture where the parties performed (possession, rent, reliance). Opposing counsel may cite stricter pre-2020 cases from other DCAs (e.g., *Skylake*, 3d DCA; *S & I Investments*, 4th DCA), but those are not 2d DCA decisions and are undercut by the Legislature’s 2020 fix and the equitable line of authority discussed above. ([Supreme Court of Florida][8])\n\n## Conclusion\n\nFlorida **does not** require two witnesses for leases executed **on or after July 1, 2020**. For older leases executed without two witnesses, arguments grounded in **Medina** (2d DCA) and **Gill** (Fla. S. Ct.)—estoppel and tolerance for curing technical witness defects—remain persuasive within the Second District. Going forward, maintain standard execution and (if recording) formatting under **§ 695.26**, but you no longer need to procure witnesses for lease documents. ([Online Sunshine][4])\n\n---\n\n**Key Authorities (selected):**\n\n* **Fla. Stat. § 689.01(1)** (2025): “no subscribing witnesses shall be required for a lease… or any instrument pertaining to a lease.” (eff. July 1, 2020). ([Online Sunshine][4])\n* **Senate Judiciary Staff Analysis, SB 1224 (Jan. 22, 2020)** (summarizing change; witness rule historically applied to lessor; equity/estoppel cases). ([The Florida Senate][5])\n* **Medina v. Orange County**, 147 So. 2d 556 (Fla. 2d DCA 1962) (witness defect cured; equity; cites **Gill** lease estoppel). ([CaseMine][1])\n* **Carroll v. Dougherty**, 355 So. 2d 843 (Fla. 2d DCA 1978) (two-witness rule does not apply to contracts to convey). ([CaseMine][2])\n* **Wickes Corp. v. Moxley**, 342 So. 2d 839 (Fla. 2d DCA 1977), **aff’d**, 356 So. 2d 785 (Fla. 1978) (no two-witness requirement for homestead mortgages post-1968). ([Leagle][3])\n* **Gill v. Livingston**, 29 So. 2d 631 (Fla. 1947) (estoppel against using one-witness defect to void lease after accepting benefits). ([CourtListener][7])\n\nIf you want, I can tailor this to a specific county/venue within the 2d DCA or draft brief-ready argument sections for either side of a legacy (pre-2020) lease dispute.\n\n[1]: https://www.casemine.com/judgement/us/59149be9add7b0493463c8b1 "MEDINA v. ORANGE COUNTY | 147 So. 2d 556 | Fla. Dist. Ct. App. | Judgment | Law | CaseMine"\n[2]: https://www.casemine.com/judgement/us/59149425add7b049345b757d "CARROLL v. DOUGHERTY | 355 So. 2d 843 | Fla. Dist. Ct. App. | Judgment | Law | CaseMine"\n[3]: https://www.leagle.com/decision/19771181342so2d83911060?utm_source=chatgpt.com "WICKES CORP. v. MOXLEY | 342 So.2d 839... | o2d83911060 - Leagle"\n[4]: https://www.leg.state.fl.us/Statutes/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute&URL=0600-0699%2F0689%2FSections%2F0689.01.html&utm_source=chatgpt.com "Statutes & Constitution :View Statutes : Online Sunshine"\n[5]: https://www.flsenate.gov/Session/Bill/2020/1224/Analyses/2020s01224.ju.PDF "2020 S0122 JU"\n[6]: https://www.leg.state.fl.us/statutes/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute&URL=0600-0699%2F0695%2FSections%2F0695.26.html&utm_source=chatgpt.com "Statutes & Constitution :View Statutes : Online Sunshine"\n[7]: https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/3389114/gill-v-livingston/?utm_source=chatgpt.com "Gill v. Livingston - CourtListener.com"\n[8]: https://supremecourt.flcourts.gov/content/download/343242/file/10-1356_JurisAns_ada.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com "SC10-1356 jurisdictional answer brief - Supreme Court of Florida"'}
